---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

export const title = "Two Years";
export const date = "2026-08-29";
export const excerpt =
  "On August 29, 2024, I was hit by a pickup truck at Thomas and Westlake. Two years later, I'm walking the length of Manhattan.";
export const tags = ["recovery", "biking", "reflection"];
---

<SiteLayout title={`${title} — Saachi K Gupta`}>
  <h1>{title}</h1>
  <p style="opacity:0.7;">{date}</p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    The ride had been good. I'd gone out with a friend — the first time biking together — and I was feeling it. The kind of afternoon where your body feels right and you don't want to stop. So instead of taking the light rail from U-District, which would have been the direct route home, I biked all the way back down to South Lake Union to take it from Westlake. More distance. More time outside. More of what the day already was.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    It was 8:25 PM. I got hit at the intersection of Thomas and Westlake. A pickup truck. What I remember from the impact: the pavement, and then a man yelling at me — loudly, the first thing I heard — and feeling scared and afraid and like I was going to be left there alone. And then thinking: it's exactly two months before my birthday. My 30th birthday.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    The pain in my lower back felt like someone had driven a fist into it. It was better when I brought my knees up. I wanted to curl my spine. I didn't know yet that my spine was fractured.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I went to Harborview. They sent me home without an MRI or a CT scan. My mom came, and she advocated for me to get imaging — and that's how, nine days after the accident, they found out I had three fractures: an L1 burst fracture in my lower spine with 50% vertebral body height loss, a fracture in my left sacral ala, and a broken tailbone. All of it missed on the first visit.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    The five months that followed felt like they could not possibly be my reality. By the numbers, August 29 to January 29: 64 X-rays of my spine, 2 ER trips, 2 CTs, 2 MRIs, 50 clinic visits, 20-plus PT sessions, 1 surgery, and 7 weeks of my mom in Seattle. The receptionists at UW knew me by name.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I'd read about women's pain getting minimized — more so for women of color — but I was not prepared for this to be my experience. I was denied imaging after the accident and sent home. It took my mom advocating for me to get the MRI. Nine days later: three fractures. That still makes me angry.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    It also, unexpectedly, cured me of being scared of medical things. When you have no choice but to show up, over and over, you stop dreading it. The only way out is through — and once you've actually lived that, it stops being a platitude.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    Nobody tells you that the administrative burden of an accident is its own injury. While I was in pain and couldn't sit on a hard surface without it being intolerable, I was simultaneously navigating a State Farm PIP claim, a separate claim against the other driver's insurance, my own Aetna coverage, work accommodation requests, parking permits, four different specialists across two hospital systems, and a personal injury attorney. My notes from that period run dozens of pages. I was taking screenshots of claim numbers and adjuster phone numbers and billing codes from a hospital bed. It is a lot to hold when you are also just trying to get out of bed without it hurting.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    It took me six weeks to feel comfortable driving again. I'd been the friend with a big car since I graduated college. That was the loss I hadn't anticipated — not the physical limitation but the independence. Not being able to get myself anywhere.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I wrote more about what coming back on skis felt like — that's in the <a href="/adventures/shejumps-ikon-pass">SheJumps post</a>. But skis were just one piece of it. The bigger thing was relearning how to be in my body and trust that it would hold.
  </p>

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">Two years out</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    Today is August 29, 2026. I'm in New York, and I'm going to walk the length of Manhattan with my friends.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    In the two years since the accident, I've done two Seafair Triathlons — which includes a bike leg, which I did not catastrophize about, which felt significant. I bike commute now, which I hadn't done before the accident. And earlier this summer, I biked up the Sunrise Road to Mount Rainier — one of the better days I've had on a bike.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    My body awareness is also, genuinely, better than it was before. All that PT — all those weeks of paying close attention to small things — left me with a fluency in my own body I didn't have at 29. I know what's tired versus what's hurt. I know when to push and when the signal is real. That's not nothing.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I'm not fragile.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I think about the choice I made that Thursday a lot. Not with regret — I keep choosing more, and that's the point. But I think about it because it's a clean example of the thing the accident took and then gave back: the willingness to take the longer way because the day is good and you're not ready for it to end.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    I'm in New York now, and I feel trepidation biking on streets I don't know yet. That part is still real. But I also know that the way I got comfortable again in Seattle was by leaning on my friends — and I can do that again here. I've done harder things.
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    Two years out. Still taking the longer way.
  </p>
</SiteLayout>
